[Proof that {lerp} and {unlerp} on the same interval are inverses
 { f(u) = unlerp(a,u,b) = (u-a)/(b-a) }
 { x(u) = lerp(a,u,b)   = (1-u)a + u b }
 
 Then we have { f(x(u)) = x(f(u)) }
 
 [Proof: I mean those are inverses because we can do algebra like this
  {(e1)
   t = f(u)
   <-> t = (u-a)/(b-a)
   <-> u = a + (b-a)t
   <-> u = x(t)
  }
  
  [NOTE The rest is generic to content of {f} and {x}
   [Proof that { f(x(t)) == t }
    If we set { u = x(t) }, due to {e1}, { t = f(u) }, so
    { t = f(u) = f(x(t)) }]
   
   [Proof that { x(f(u)) == u } is the same deal!]]
  ]
 ]

[Symmetry of Bezier curves
 If we reverse the order of the control points,
 obtaining { c = reverse(b) } what happens? 
 
 We can prove { (eq_5_11): b(t) = c(1-t) }
 
 [#done It follows directly from this Lemma:
  { B__i^n(1-t) == B__(n-i)^n(t) }
  because { B__i^n = binom(n,i) t^i (1-t)^(n-i) }
  ]
 ]

[Invariance under barycentric combination
 The weighted average (lerp) two of Bezier curves is another Bezier curve
 {
  (alpha b + beta c)(t)
  = sum(i in [0,n],
        B__i^n (alpha b__i + beta c__i))
  = alpha b(t) + beta c(t)
 }
 ]

[Linear precision
 [Lemma: { sum(i in [0,n],  i B__i^n(t)) = n t }
  Don't believe me? Try @linear_precision_identity_test
  
  [Proof... thanks to Grok, and the source that it ~~stole from~~ trained on
   {
    sum(i in [0,n],  i/n B__i^n(t))
    = sum(i in [0,n],  i/n binom(n,i) (1-t)^(n-i) t^i)
    // NOTE The term at i=0 doesn't do anything, so we can ignore it
    = sum(i in [1,n],  i/n binom(n,i) (1-t)^(n-i) t^i)
    // due to @binomial_shift
    = sum(i in [1,n],  binom(n-1, i-1) (1-t)^(n-i) t^i)
    // change i to i+1
    = sum(i in [0,n-1],  binom(n-1, i) (1-t)^(n-1-i) t^(i+1))
    = t sum(i in [0,n-1],  B__i^(n-1))
    //... this is incredible!
    = t
   }
   ]
  
  [A more straight-forward proof (source: me)
   {
    sum(i in [0,n],  i B__i^n(t))
    // using @bernstein_lerp (the i=0 case doesn't matter)
    = sum(i in [1,n],  i lerp(B__i^(n-1)(t),
                              t,
                              B__(i-1)^(n-1)(t)))
    = ((1-t) sum(i in [1,n], i B__i    ^(n-1)(t)) +
       t     sum(i in [1,n], i B__(i-1)^(n-1)(t)))
    {// Detour on the first term
     sum(i in [1,n], i B__i^(n-1)(t))
     = sum(i in [1,n-1], i B__i^(n-1)(t)) + n B__n^(n-1)(t)
     // second term is 0 :>, also i can run from 0 for the first term
     = sum(i in [0,n-1], i B__i^(n-1)(t))
     // using induction hypothesis
     = (n-1) t
    }
    {// Detour on the second term!
     sum(i in [1,n],  i B__(i-1)^(n-1)(t))
     // change i to i+1
     = sum(i in [0,n-1], (i+1) B__i^(n-1)(t))
     // don't be afraid of the "i+1"
     = sum(i in [0,n-1], (i B__i^(n-1)(t) +
                          B__i^(n-1)(t)))
     // using partition of unity, and induction hypothesis
     = 1 + (n-1) t
    }
    // NOTE coming back
    = (1-t) (n-1) t + t (1+(n-1)t)
    = lerp((n-1) t, t, 1+(n-1)t)
    = (n-1) t + t
    = n t
   }
   ]
  ]
 
 [Suppose the control points {b__i} are uniformly distributed on the line segment {p,q}
  { b__i = (1-i/n)p + i/n q; i in [0,n] }
  Then what is the output?
  {
   b(t)
   = sum(i in [0,n], B__i^n(t) b__i)
   = sum(i in [0,n], B__i^n(t) ((1-i/n)p + i/n q))
   // Using the insane identity above...
   = (1-t)p + t q
  }
  ]
 ]

[So we know that lerp is an affine map, so we could apply it to a vector,
 and the result would be... a vector.
 See @affine_map_R3 for reason why you can do this
 We can compute it as follows
 {
  l(t)      = lerp(a,t,b)
  l(vec(v)) = l(v) - l(0) = (b-a)v
 }
 In hindsight, it is obvious because { l(t) = (b-a)t + a },
 which is of the linear-transform-and-offset form,
 like all affine maps. But I wasn't thinking about that.
 ]

[;Blossom_Vector Blossoms with vector argument
 If the blossomer sees a vector {vec(v)},
 We define the result to be the standard vector extension you saw in @affine_map_vector
 {
  b[vec(v),s,t]
  = lerp(b[0,s,t], vec(v), b[1,s,t])
  = lerp(b[0,s,t], v-0, b[1,s,t])
  = b[v,s,t] - b[0,s,t]  // @affine_map_vector
 }
 i.e it's just the same linear interpolation (with vector argument),
 
 [We have this fact
  {lerp_vector_fact:
   lerp(a-b, t, c-d)
   = (1-t)(a-b) + t(c-d)
   = (1-t)a + t c - ((1-t)b + t d)
   = lerp(a,t,c) - lerp(b,t,d)
  }
  ]
 
 {symmetry_one_vector}
 If {vec(v)} was the second argument, we could still promote it to be the first
 {
  b[s, vec(v)]
  = lerp(b[0,vec(v)], s, b[1,vec(v)])
  = lerp(b[0,v] - b[0,0], s,
         b[1,v] - b[1,0])
  using(lerp_vector_fact)
  = lerp(b[0,v],s,b[1,v]) - lerp(b[0,0],s,b[1,0])
  = b[s,v] - b[s,0]
  = b[vec(v),s]
 }
 
 [#done What if the vector argument was somewhere else?
  What if there are multiple vector arguments?
  
  We can just look at the first argument, and go along the way
  { b[vec(v),*] = b[v,*] - b[0,*] }
  [#done_kinda We have to establish the type of the output
   The output type is either a point or a vector
   If at least one argument is a vector, the result is a vector
   [1. If the first argument is a vector, then the result is a subtraction. Cases:
    1a. Both operands are points, then the result is a vector
    2a. Both operands are vectors, then the result is a vector
    NOTE There is no case in which one is a vector and the other is point,
    because both blossoms have the same arguments]
   [2. If the first argument is a point, the result is a lerp. Cases:
    1a. Both operands are points, the result is a point
    2a. Both operands are vectors, the result is a vector
    Again, there's no mixing vectors and points
    ]
   ]
  ]
 
 [#done {symmetry_two_vectors} We can swap two vectors
  {
   b[vec(v), vec(w)]
   = b[v, vec(w)] - b[0, vec(w)]
   using (symmetry_one_vector)
   = b[v,w] - b[v,0] - b[0,w] + b[0,0]
   = b[v,w] - b[0,w] - (b[v,0] - b[0,0])
   = b[w, vec(v)] - (b[0, vec(v)])
   = b[vec(w), vec(v)]
  }
  ]
 
