[;thm_5_15 Let {t} be a point and {vec(v)} be a vector in 1D space
 We have { b[(t+vec(v))^(<n>)] = sum(i in [0,n],
                                     binom(n,i) b[t^(<n-i>), vec(v)^(<i>)]) }
 @thm_5_15_test Tested!
 
 [{L1024} { b[t+vec(v),*] = b[t,*] + b[vec(v),*] },
  due to the affine nature of lerp (more at @fact_336)]
 
 [Ok let's do the computation "by-hand"
  { b[t+vec(v), t+vec(v)] }
  {
   rhs
   = (1 b[vec(v),vec(v)] +
      2 b[t,vec(v)] +
      1 b[t,t])
   = (b[vec(v),vec(v)] + b[t,vec(v)]) + (b[t,vec(v)] + b[t,t])
   = b[t+vec(v), vec(v)] + b[t,t+vec(v)]
   = b[t+vec(v), t+vec(v)]
  }
  ]
 
 [Refer to @Leibniz_formula for the ideas of this proof
  {
   b[t+vec(v) times n+1]
   = b[t+vec(v)][t+vec(v) times n]
   // Induction hypothesis
   = sum(i in [0,n],
         binom(n,i) b[t+vec(v), t times n-i, vec(v) times i])
   using (L1024)
   = (sum(i in [0,n], binom(n,i) b[t times n+1-i, vec(v) times i]) +
      sum(i in [0,n], binom(n,i) b[t times n-i, vec(v) times i+1]))
   // massaging the second sum: i -> i-1
   = (sum(i in [0,n], binom(n,i) b[t times n+1-i, vec(v) times i]) +
      sum(i in [1,n+1], binom(n,i-1) b[t times n+1-i, vec(v) times i]))
   // Massaging the sum ranges so they match
   = (sum(i in [0,n+1], binom(n,i)   b[t times n+1-i, vec(v) times i]) +
      sum(i in [0,n+1], binom(n,i-1) b[t times n+1-i, vec(v) times i]))
   // Collapsing them back
   = sum(i in [0,n+1], binom(n+1,i) b[t times n+1-i, vec(v) times i])
   // #qed
  }
  ]
 
 [NOTE In the CAGD book, I bet money that the author didn't really prove this.
  Leibniz's formula does NOT cover this case!
  He probably plugged in {alpha=beta=1} and called it a day...
  ]
 ]

[#done Pseudolocal control
 The bernstein polynomial {B__i^n} (the weight of the control point {b__i})
 has one only one maximum, and attains it at {t = i/n}
 { B__i^n(t) = binom(n,i) (1-t)^(n-i) t^i }
 What's the derivative of this thing?
 {
  diff(B__i^n(t), t)
  = binom(n,i) diff((1-t)^(n-i) t^i, t)
  // NOTE Drop the useless binomial scale
  diff((1-t)^(n-i) t^i, t)
  = (1-t)^(n-i) diff(t^i, t) + t^i diff((1-t)^(n-i), t)
  = (1-t)^(n-i) i t^(i-1) + t^i diff((1-t)^(n-i), t)
 }
 
 [Rusty with calculus much? what is this?
  { diff((1-t)^(n-i), t) }
  Suppose {u = (1-t)}
  then the chain rule says that
  {
   diff((1-t)^(n-i), t)
   = diff(u^(n-i), u) diff(1-t, t)
   = (n-i) u^(n-i-1) * (-1)
   = (i-n) (1-t)^(n-i-1)
  }
  ]
 
 so we can get back to the differentiation
 {
  diff((1-t)^(n-i) t^i, t)
  = i (1-t)^(n-i) t^(i-1) + (i-n) (1-t)^(n-i-1) t^i
  = (1-t)^(n-i-1) t^(i-1) (i (1-t) + (i-n) t)
  = (1-t)^(n-i-1) t^(i-1) (i - n t)
 }
 The {i - n t} factor is the key: the rest is always positive,
 so the sign of this derivative is that of {i - n t},
 it is positive on {[0,i/n]}, and negative on {[i/n,1]}
 ]

[;bezier_derivative The derivative of the curve {x(t) = b^n(t)} is defined as
 {
  diff(x,t)
  = lim(vec(h) -> vec(0),
        frac(1, abs(vec(h))) (x(t+vec(h)) - x(t)))
 }
 
 [NOTE Again, I don't understand the {abs(vec(h))} notation
  I think it's probably just signed? So it's just {h}, loosely speaking
  [Also, we write it that way so it's easier to parse]]
 
 Invoking @thm_5_15, we have
 {
  diff(x,t)
  = lim(vec(h) -> vec(0),
        (1 / abs(vec(h)))
        (sum(i in [0,n],
             binom(n,i) b[t^(<n-i>), vec(v)^(<i>)])
         - b[t^(<n>)]))
 }
 For {i=0}, two terms {b[t^(<n>)]} cancel (the "point" part),
 leaving us with the vector part
 {
  diff(x,t)
  = lim(vec(h) -> vec(0),
        (1 / abs(vec(h)))
        (sum(i in [1,n],
             binom(n,i) b[t^(<n-i>), vec(h)^(<i>)])))
  // Expanding things out
  = lim(vec(h) -> vec(0),
        (1 / abs(vec(h)))
        (n b[t^(<n-1>), vec(h)] +
         binom(n,2) b[t^(<n-2>), vec(h), vec(h)] +
         ...))
 }
 Notice that {x lerp(a, vec(v), b) = lerp(a, x v, b)},
 because {lerp} is an affine map, it preserves scale.
 So when we put {1/abs(vec(h))} into play
 {
  diff(x, t)
  = lim(vec(h) -> vec(0),
        (n b[t^(<n-1>), vec(h) / abs(vec(h))] +
         binom(n,2) b[t^(<n-2>), vec(h) / abs(vec(h)), vec(h)] +
         ...))
 }
 
 We observe that {vec(h) / abs(vec(h)) = vec(1)}.
 Also when {vec(h)} goes to {vec(0)},
 {binom(n,2) b[t^(<n-2>), vec(h) / abs(vec(h)), vec(h)]} goes to zero
 (because {lerp(vec(*), vec(0), vec(*)) = vec(0)}).
 Same goes for all the terms after that, so we have
 
 { (eq_5_17)
  diff(x,t) = n b[t times n-1, vec(1)] }
 [Tested in @bezier_derivative_test]
 
 [NOTE In the cubic case,
  At {t=0}, the derivative is 3 times {b__1 - b__0}
  At {t=1}, the derivative is 3 times {b__3 - b__2}]
 
 [We can interpret {eq_5_17} in two ways,
  the first way is to perform one de Casteljau step with {vec(1)},
  and then {n-1} steps with {t}, as an equation
  {
   newton_diff(x,t) = n sum(j in [0,n-1],
                            B__j^(n-1)(t) (b__(j+1) - b__j))
  }
  We can simply it using the forward difference operator {Delta}
  {
   newton_diff(x,t) = n sum(j in [0,n-1],
                            B__j^(n-1)(t) Delta b__j);
   Delta b__j in bb(R)^3
  }
  
  {Delta b__j} (called the "hodograph") is a curve of degree {n-1},
  consisting of vectors.
  NOTE I just realized that vector lives in {bb(R)^3}, points in {E^3}
  
  [NOTE The hodograph does not change if you translate the original curve {b}]
  ]
 
 [For the second interpretation
  First we do {n-1} de Casteljau steps with {t},
  then we subtract the first remaining points from the second
  { newton_diff(x,t) = n (b__1^(n-1)(t) - b__0^(n-1)(t)) }
  ]
 ]

[
 {(eq_5_10)
  
  B__i^n(0) = (i == 0)
  B__i^n(1) = (i == n)
 }
 where {(a == b)} returns 1 for {a = b}, 0 otherwise (just like computer code)
 
 Why: recall the definition of the Bernstein polynomials
 { B__i^n(t) = binom(n,i) t^i (1-t)^(n-i) }
 so anything having {t} (i.e {i > 0}) would vanish, except { B__0^n(0) = 1 }
 ]

[;bezier_higher_derivative
 [#done Higher derivatives follow the same pattern in @bezier_derivative
  {eq_5_21:
   x^((r))(t) = falling_factorial(n,r) b[t times n-r, vec(1) times r] }
  
  How do to see that? {eq_5_17} serves as the base case,
  { x^((1))(t) = falling_factorial(n,1) b[t times n-1, vec(1)] }
  
  Then the inductive step is as follows (example for {r=1}, but easily generalized)
  {
   x^((2))(t)
   = diff(x^((1))(t), t)
   = diff(n b[t times n-1, vec(1)](t), t)
   // NOTE b[vec(1)] is just another curve of degree n-1.
   = diff(n b[vec(1)][t times n-1](t), t)
   = n diff(b[vec(1)][t times n-1](t), t)
   // NOTE Applying the base step
   = n (n-1) diff(b[vec(1), vec(1), t times n-2](t), t)
   // #qed
  }
  ]
 
 [To compute these higher derivatives from Bezier points,
  we first generalize the forward difference operator {Delta},
  the [iterated forward difference iterator {Delta^r}] is defined by
  {
   0. Delta^0 b__j     = b__j
   1. Delta^(r+1) b__j = Delta^r b__(j+1) - Delta^r b__j
  }
  
  [Let's study the expanded coefficients
   {
    Delta^0 b__0 = b__0
    Delta^1 b__0 = b__1 - b__0  // NOTE agrees with our previous definition
    Delta^2 b__0 = b__2 - 2 b__1 + b__0
    Delta^3 b__0 = b__3 - 3 b__2 + 3 b__1 - b__0
   }
   The coefficients form a Pascal-like triangle
   
   How do you write down this pattern?
   {(eq_5_23):
    Delta^r b__0 = sum__(j=0)^r (-1)^(r-j) binom(r,j) b__j }
   
   Let's prove it!
   { Delta^(r+1) b__0 = Delta^r b__1 - Delta^r b__0
    = (sum__(j=0)^r (-1)^(r-j) binom(r,j) b__(j+1)
       - sum__(j=0)^r (-1)^(r-j) binom(r,j) b__j)
    // Massage the first sum j -> j-1
    = (sum__(j=1)^(r+1) (-1)^(r-(j-1)) binom(r,(j-1)) b__(j-1+1)
       - sum__(j=0)^r (-1)^(r-j) binom(r,j) b__j)
    // Actually the first sum could start at j=0
    // Also flip the sign of the second sum
    = (sum__(j=0)^(r+1) (-1)^(r+1-j) binom(r,j-1) b__j
       +
       sum__(j=0)^r (-1)^(r+1-j) binom(r,j) b__j)
    // The second sum could run to j=r+1
    = (sum__(j=0)^(r+1) (-1)^(r+1-j) binom(r,j-1) b__j
       +
       sum__(j=0)^(r+1) (-1)^(r+1-j) binom(r,j) b__j)
    // Ok, let's combine the sums! (Using @Pascals_rule)
    = sum__(j=0)^(r+1) (-1)^(r+1-j) binom(r+1,j) b__j
   }
   ]
  ]
 
 [#done The {r}-th derivative of a Bezier curve is now given by
  {eq_5_24:
   
   x^((r))(t) =
   falling_factorial(n,r) sum__(j=0)^(n-r) B__j^(n-r)(t) Delta^r b__j
  }
  
  Ok just what is that? For {r=1}
  {
   newton_diff(x,t)
   = n sum__(j=0)^(n-1) B__j^(n-1)(t) Delta b__j
  }
  
  So according to {eq_5_21}, we only need to prove that 
  { b[t times n-r, vec(1) times r] =
   sum__(j=0)^(n-r) B__j^(n-r)(t) Delta^r b__j }
  
  Which is true if { (b[vec(1) times r])__j = Delta^r b__j }
  [Well let's see,
   1. { b[vec(1)]__j = b__(j+1) - b__j = Delta b__j }, true!
   2. { b[vec(1) times 2] = b[vec(1)]__(j+1) - b[vec(1)]__j
    = Delta b__(j+1) - Delta b__j }, true!
   ]
  so yeah, #qed
  ]
 
 [{eq_5_25:}
  When {t=0}, we have according to {eq_5_24} and {eq_5_10}
  { x^((r))(0) = falling_factorial(n,r) Delta^r b__0 }
  Similarly for {t=1}
  { x^((r))(1) = falling_factorial(n,r) Delta^r b__(n-r) }
  
  Another way to see this: { b[vec(1)] times r } leaves you with the list
  { [Delta^r b__0, Delta^r b__1, ... Delta^r b__(r-n)] }
  and recall that the Bezier curve interpolates the endpoints.
  
  The r-th derivative of a Bezier curve at an endpoint depends only on the r+1
  points near that endpoint.
  For {r=0}, we retrieve the endpoint interpolation property.
  ]
 
 [@Taylor_series situation
  This is the Taylor series of {x(t)} centered at 0
  { x(t) = sum__(i=0)^n frac(1,i!) x^((i))(0) t^i }
  
  We know how {x^((i))(0)} works from {eq_5_25}, plugging that in
  {eq_5_27
   x(t) = sum__(i=0)^n binom(n,i) Delta^i b__0 t^i }
  [NOTE Tested at @eq_5_27_test]
  
  NOTE This is called the "monomial" form, because there's only {t^i},
  whereas the Bernstein polynomial contains also {(1-t)^(n-i)}
  
  [#done Just for fun, let's equate directly {eq_5_27} and @Bezier_Bernstein
   (turned out it wasn't really fun...)
   {
    sum__(i=0)^n binom(n,i) Delta^i b__0 t^i
    vs.
    sum__(i=0)^n B__i^n(t) b__i
   }
   
   Well, expanding the [forward difference] is the move...
   (induction on {n} won't work)
   {
    sum__(i=0)^n binom(n,i) Delta^i b__0 t^i
    = sum__(i=0)^n binom(n,i) (sum__(j=0)^i (-1)^(i-j) binom(i,j) b__j) t^i
    
    = sum__(i=0)^n (sum__(j=0)^n (-1)^(i-j) binom(n,i) t^i binom(i,j) b__j)
    // Switch place the variables (transposing the table of i,j)
    = sum__(j=0)^n (sum__(i=0)^n (-1)^(i-j) binom(n,i) binom(i,j) t^i) b__j
    // to be continued...
   }
   Let's focus solely on the coefficients of {b__j}
   {
    sum__(i=0)^n (-1)^(i-j) binom(n,i) binom(i,j) t^i
    // actually i only runs from j
    = sum__(i=j)^n (-1)^(i-j) binom(n,i) binom(i,j) t^i
    // using @binomial_chain_rule
    = sum__(i=j)^n (-1)^(i-j) binom(n,j) binom(n-j,i-j) t^i
    = binom(n,j) sum__(i=j)^n (-1)^(i-j) binom(n-j,i-j) t^i
    // change the range of i
    = binom(n,j) sum__(i=0)^(n-j) (-1)^(i) binom(n-j,i) t^(i+j)
    = binom(n,j) t^j (sum__(i=0)^(n-j) (-t)^i binom(n-j,i))
    {// Simple application of the @binomial_theorem gives...
     (1-t)^n = sum__(i=0)^n binom(n,i) (-t)^i
    }
    = binom(n,j) t^j (1-t)^(n-j)
    = B__j^n(t)
   }
   and that's our familiar Bernstein polynomial... it's been a long road, but #qed.
   ]
  ]
 ]

[If {x(t)} is defined over an interval {[a,b]}, {eq_5_17} becomes
 { eq_5_28: frac(d x, d t) = frac(n,b-a) b[frac(t-a, b-a) times n-1, vec(1)] }
 
 Rephrase: Suppose { t = a+(b-a)u } and {x = b^n(u)},
 Meaning that the curve {b(t)} interpolates
 the first control point when {t=a -> u=0},
 and the last control point at {t=b -> u=1}.
 
 From @bezier_derivative We already have
 { frac(d x, d u) = n b[u times n-1, vec(1)] }
 So according to the @chain_rule,
 {
  frac(d x, d t) = frac(d x, d u) frac(d u, d t)
  { frac(d u, d t) = frac(1,(b-a)) }
  -> frac(d x, d t) = frac(n,b-a) b[u times n-1, vec(1)]
 }
 Tested in @test_5_28
 [NOTE If I understood the statement correctly, then it is wrong!
  However I'm not sure... without context there really is no way to know]
 ]

[We already know that the Bezier curve defined
 over the interval {[0,c]} (meaning that { c(t) = b(t c) })
 has control points
 { c__i = b__0^i(c) }
 
 What can we say about the curve defined over the interval {[c,1]}?
 Well let's call that curve {e}, by definition
 { e(t) = b(lerp(c,t,1)) = b((1-t)c + t) }
 we will prove
 { e__j = b__j^(n-j) }
 
 [#fail By the book: Because of the symmetry property {eq_5_11}
  it follows that the control vertices of the part
  corresponding to {[c,1]} (let's call that {e})
  are given by the {b__j^(n-j)}
  [NOTE I don't believe it! Prove it!]]
 
 [#done Can we even prove this property with what we know?
  Suppose we have a cubic curve {b}, then {e} would be
  {
   e = [b[c,c,c], b[c,c,1], b[c,1,1], b[1,1,1]]
   = [b__0^3, b__1^2, b__2^1, b__3^0]
   -> e__j = b__j^(n-j)
  }
  ]
 
 [What can we say about the curve defined over the interval {[1,d]}?
  We know that
  { d__j = [1 times n-j, d times j] = b__(n-j)^j
   == e__(n-j) }
  Yeah that checks out! The symmetric property of {eq_5_11}!
  And actually that's also another way you can prove the symmetric property]
 ]

[Blossom and polar
 Let {p__1 = b[t__1]}, that is, {b} after one de Casteljau step
 { p__1(t)
  = sum(i in [0,n-1],
        B__i^(n-1)(t) (1-t__1)b__i + (t__1 b__(i+1)))
  = (sum(i in [0,n-1],
         B__i^(n-1)(t) ((1-t__1)b__i + (t__1 b__(i+1)) - b__i^1(t))) +
     sum(i in [0,n-1],
         B__i^(n-1)(t) b__i^1(t)))
  = (sum(i in [0,n-1],
         B__i^(n-1)(t) ((1-t__1)b__i + t__1 b__(i+1) -
                        ((1-t)b__i + t b__(i+1)))) +
     b(t))
  // NOTE(kv) Long algebra here...
  = ((t__1 - t) sum(i in [0,n-1],
                    B__i^(n-1)(t) (b__(i+1) - b__i)) +
     b(t))
 }
 Therefore, (pingback to {eq_5_17})
 { (eq_5_31)
  p__1(t) = b(t) + frac(t__1-t, n) frac(d b(t), d t)
 }
 The polynomial {p__1} is called the [first polar] of {b(t)} with respect to {t__1}
 You can this working at @figure_polar_form
 ]
